  "result": "All five tests pass.\n\nImplemented `ratelimit/limiter.py` as a token bucket (Approach A). Each key stores a token count and a last-refill timestamp. Tokens refill at `limit / window_seconds` per second, capped at `limit`, and each allowed call consumes one. The constructor rejects a non-positive limit or window, and a small epsilon in the availability check guards against floating-point drift when an elapsed interval is exactly one token's worth.\n\n```\n$ python -m pytest -q\n.....                                                                    [100%]\n5 passed in 0.03s\n```\n\nI chose the token bucket because it needs only two numbers per key with O(1) time and memory regardless of the limit, whereas the sliding-window log grows with `limit` and does per-call pruning. The spec's required behaviour is fully satisfied by either, so the cheaper, simpler state model won.",
